## TL;DR
```bash
helm install external-secrets \
  {{ $releases }}/download/<tag>/external-secrets-<version>.tgz \
  --namespace external-secrets --create-namespace
```

## Installing the Chart
Install the chart attached to a release of [NaverCloudPlatform/external-secrets]({{ $releases }}): `<tag>` is the release tag, for example `v2.14.0`, and `<version>` is the same without the `v`. That chart carries the CRDs with the `ncloud` provider's fields and pins the image of its release. Keep the release name and the namespace `external-secrets`, which the NKS add-on uses too.

Do not install the upstream chart from `https://charts.external-secrets.io`: its CRDs lack the `ncloud` fields, so admission rejects `ncloud` stores. A checkout of `deploy/charts/external-secrets` is not installable as it is either, because its CRD templates are generated when the release chart is packaged.

### Custom Resources
By default, the chart will install external-secrets CRDs, this can be controlled with `installCRDs` value.

## Uninstalling the Chart
To uninstall the `{{ template "chart.name" . }}` deployment:
```bash
helm uninstall {{ template "chart.name" . }} --namespace external-secrets
```
The command removes the Kubernetes components associated with the chart and deletes the release. It keeps the CRDs the chart installed, and every store and `ExternalSecret` with them, because `crds.annotations` marks them `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep` by default.

Helm reads the mark from the manifest it stored for the release, so a release without it loses them: one that set the annotation to `null`, or one upgraded with `--reuse-values` from a chart that did not have the default. To check, count the CRDs that are kept:
```bash
helm get manifest {{ template "chart.name" . }} --namespace external-secrets | grep -c 'helm.sh/resource-policy: keep'
```
If it prints 0, upgrade before uninstalling, with the chart version the release already runs and `--reuse-values --set 'crds.annotations.helm\.sh/resource-policy=keep'`. Without `--reuse-values`, a `--set` drops the values the release was given before. With it, Helm also keeps the defaults the release runs with, so a newer chart given here would not bring its image tag.

To remove the CRDs as well, delete them with `kubectl` before uninstalling, while the controller is still running: it puts finalizers on what it manages, every `ExternalSecret` among them, and only it removes them. After the uninstall a resource that carries one cannot be deleted, so a CRD stays in `Terminating`, and so does a namespace that holds such a resource, until a controller runs again.

The NKS add-on does not install over this release. To move to it, uninstall the release and then install the add-on.

